# Dev only: LAN hosts (comma-separated) allowed to open the dev server, for
# phone/tablet testing — e.g. DEV_ORIGINS=192.168.1.20
DEV_ORIGINS=

# DFO engine base URL (Search, Datasets and graph APIs). Required: the server
# refuses to start in production without it, and every call to those APIs
# fails in dev. The dev apis service admits only callers with
# roles/run.invoker, so local work reaches it through a proxy that signs each
# call with your gcloud login (docs/development.md, "Reach the dev APIs"):
#   gcloud run services proxy dfo-dev-apis --region northamerica-northeast1 \
#     --project dev-fin-observatory --port 8083
SEARCH_API_BASE=http://localhost:8083

# Browser-side override for the Search API base. Empty means same-origin
# (the app proxies /api/search/* and /api/datasets/*) — set this only to hit
# a remote engine origin directly from the browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SEARCH_API_BASE=

# Studio agent (the dfo-ai harness). The proxy under /api/studio/* sends the
# token as a bearer; without it every turn answers 401. Defaults to a harness
# on localhost:8080 when the base is unset.
STUDIO_API_BASE=
STUDIO_TOKEN=

# Accounts and saved conversations (Postgres). Local development only --
# run `just db-up && just db-migrate` first, then this connects as the
# `website_runtime` role db/migrate-local.ts creates. On a Postgres you
# already run instead of the container, change the port here to match
# PGPORT (docs/development.md, "Use a Postgres you already run").
# Deployed services don't read DATABASE_URL at all; they connect to Cloud SQL via
# WEBSITE_DB_INSTANCE_CONNECTION_NAME, WEBSITE_DB_USER, and WEBSITE_DB_NAME
# instead (set by dfo-infra). The schema lives in db/schema.ts; migrations
# are generated with `pnpm db:generate`.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://website_runtime:website_runtime_dev_password@localhost:5433/website

# Better Auth. The secret signs sessions (32+ random bytes, e.g.
# `openssl rand -base64 32`); the URL is this app's own public origin.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Sign-in providers (Better Auth social sign-in). There are no password
# accounts. Each pair is optional: a provider without keys isn't offered on
# /login. Callback URL to register with every provider:
#   <BETTER_AUTH_URL>/api/auth/callback/<google|github|microsoft>
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The Graph Explorer (/graph-explorer, src/lib/auth/reader.ts) admits a
# signed-in user whose verified email's domain is exactly one of these,
# comma-separated, e.g. example.org,partner.example. Unset or empty admits
# nobody.
GRAPH_EXPLORER_EMAIL_DOMAINS=

# Usage events of these users carry internal_eng: true (src/lib/events/internalEng.ts,
# docs/analytics.md). Comma-separated, each an email address or a bare domain,
# e.g. dev@example.org,example.org. Unset or empty marks nobody.
INTERNAL_ENG_EMAILS=

# Email sign-in: /login emails a six-digit code (src/lib/auth/emailSignIn.ts).
# On only when these three and a mail transport (below) are all set, so no
# code is sent without the Turnstile bot check. AUTH_EMAIL_FROM is the From header,
# e.g. DFO Studio <studio@developmentfinance.org>. Turnstile keys come from
# the Cloudflare dashboard; locally, Cloudflare's always-pass test keys work:
# site 1x00000000000000000000AA, secret 1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA.
AUTH_EMAIL_FROM=
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# Feedback form (POST /api/feedback, docs/feedback.md). Submissions are
# stored via DATABASE_URL above; each sink below is optional and off when
# unset. The Slack webhook posts each submission to a channel (deployed:
# mounted from Secret Manager by dfo-infra; values that aren't a
# hooks.slack.com URL mean off). Submissions are emailed to FEEDBACK_TO
# through the mail transport below.
FEEDBACK_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
FEEDBACK_FROM=
FEEDBACK_TO=

# Mail transport, for sign-in codes and feedback (src/lib/mail/settings.ts).
# Deployed environments send through the Amazon SES v2 API. AWS STS trades
# the Cloud Run service account's Google ID token, minted for the audience
# SES_TOKEN_AUDIENCE, for temporary credentials for the IAM role
# SES_ROLE_ARN, whose trust policy names that service account and audience.
# No AWS key is stored. SES_REGION is the SES account's region, e.g.
# us-east-1. Setting some of the three but not all turns mail off. The role
# sends only as the address its policy names, studio@developmentfinance.org,
# so AUTH_EMAIL_FROM and FEEDBACK_FROM use that address in the same case. A
# display name in front of it is fine.
SES_ROLE_ARN=
SES_REGION=
SES_TOKEN_AUDIENCE=
# Local work uses SMTP against a mail catcher (e.g. Mailpit), used only when
# the SES settings are unset. The connection must use TLS 1.2 or later, over
# STARTTLS (port 587) unless SMTP_SECURE=true.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_SECURE=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# Sentry (errors, browser + server; docs/analytics.md). Off without a DSN.
# Usage events need no key — they land in the `event` table.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=
# Build only, for source-map upload (Sentry -> Settings -> Auth Tokens).
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# --- Platform proxy -------------------------------------------------------------
# The DFO APIs and the MCP servers are mirrored 1:1 under this site's domain
# (/api/search|datasets|graph/* -> dfo-apis, /api/studio/* -> dfo-ai, and
# /mcp -> dfo-ai's combined MCP server at /mcp/). /api/* and /mcp have a
# per-IP rate limit, counted in the website database (table limit_counter).
# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_S=60
# RATE_LIMIT_API=120
# The Datasets API's routes below /api/v1/datasets/, which a page's live
# reads spend, have their own count.
# RATE_LIMIT_DATASETS=300
# RATE_LIMIT_PIN=10
# /mcp is higher because a vendor's address can carry many users.
# RATE_LIMIT_MCP=300
# A call with a valid API key (`Authorization: Bearer dfo_...` or `X-API-Key`)
# to the API (/api/v1/*, /openapi.json, /codes), the Datasets API's routes
# included, is counted against the key's account instead of its address: this
# many calls per window on each of the two counts, for an account with no
# limit of its own (user.api_rate_limit). A key sent to /mcp or the Studio is
# ignored.
# RATE_LIMIT_KEYED=600
# Each account also has a daily quota of API calls, counted per UTC day across
# all its keys, for an account with no quota of its own (user.api_daily_limit).
# Refused calls count. An operator may also cap one key lower
# (apikey.daily_limit).
# API_KEY_DAILY_LIMIT=75000
# How long, in seconds, the proxy caches a key's lookup (default 60), so a
# change to a key's limits or a deletion takes up to this long to apply. 0
# turns the cache off and reads the key on every call, which suits a demo.
# API_KEY_CACHE_S=60
# Emergency switch: false strips keys and counts every call by address.
# API_KEYS_ENABLED=true
# Secrets for the signed tag at the end of every key (src/lib/auth/keyTag),
# comma-separated, each at least 32 characters (`openssl rand -hex 32`). The
# first signs new keys; every one verifies. A dedicated secret per
# environment, never BETTER_AUTH_SECRET. Rotate by adding the new secret last,
# then moving it first in a later revision; removing one voids its keys.
# Required in production. Unset in dev: keys can be neither made nor used.
API_KEY_TAG_SECRETS=
# How many API keys one account may hold in the API keys panel (default 5).
# Housekeeping, not security: the limits and the daily quota are per account,
# so a second key adds no calls.
# API_KEYS_MAX_PER_ACCOUNT=5
# Who may open the user management page (/admin/api-keys), which lists every
# account's keys, makes and deletes keys and sets an account's limit. Comma-
# separated emails, matched without regard to case against a signed-in user
# whose email is verified. Everyone else gets a 404. Unset or empty: no
# admins, so the page is closed to all.
# ADMIN_EMAILS=
# The harness's own token for its MCP server (MCP_API_TOKEN on dfo-ai);
# attached by the proxy so outside MCP clients don't need it. Unset: the
# caller's own Authorization header is passed through.
MCP_API_TOKEN=
# /mcp names each caller to dfo-ai, and the dfo-apis proxy to dfo-apis, as
# x-dfo-caller: ip:<digest>, an HMAC-SHA-256 of the client address (an IPv6
# address as its /64) keyed by this secret (e.g. openssl rand -hex 32). A
# dfo-apis call with an API key carries user:<digest> of the account instead.
# It must equal dfo-apis's DFO_CALLER_HMAC_KEY. If unset, no caller is sent and
# the site logs one warning.
CALLER_HMAC_KEY=
# The secret dfo-apis checks before it trusts x-dfo-caller (its
# DFO_CALLER_PRINCIPAL), sent as x-dfo-caller-principal on every dfo-apis
# call. If unset, the site sends dfo-apis neither header and logs one warning,
# and dfo-apis counts the whole site as one caller.
DFO_CALLER_PRINCIPAL=
# Keys the x-dfo-account digest the Studio proxy sends the harness on turn and
# interrupt calls for a signed-in user: HMAC-SHA-256 of the user id, the same
# `user_hash` the event export writes (db/event-export.ts). It must equal the
# export job's EXPORT_ID_HMAC_KEY. If unset, no account is sent and the site
# logs one warning.
EXPORT_ID_HMAC_KEY=
# Which X-Forwarded-For entry is the client, counted from the right. 0 (the
# default) is the rightmost, the one Cloud Run's front end appends; add one
# for each load balancer in front of the service.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0
# OpenAI's domain-verification token for the app directory. GET
# /.well-known/openai-apps-challenge returns it as plain text, and 404 while
# it is unset.
OPENAI_APPS_CHALLENGE=
